 Open Market, Inc. First to Offer Complete
 *****************************************
End-to-End Solution for Electronic Commerce
*******************************************

 New Company Introduces StoreBuilder(tm) Kit and System
 to Enable Merchants to Easily Build and Manage On-line
 Storefronts 

CAMBRIDGE, Mass., October 24, 1994 - Open Market, Inc., a
new company dedicated to providing complete end-to-end
solutions for electronic commerce, today announced a
comprehensive set of products and services intended to make it
easy for companies and individuals to establish a commercial
presence on the Internet. 

Headquartered in Cambridge, Mass., Open Market, Inc. (OMI) is
the first company to offer merchants a complete electronic
infrastructure for establishing and managing a business on the
Internet, including software that enables a merchant to build an
on-line store, product creation tools, and a seamless and secure
payment system for buying and selling products and services
on-line. In addition, Open Market's system provides Internet
shoppers with new ways to browse, search for and purchase
goods and services offered on the Internet. 

A key element of Open Market's solution is StoreBuilder(tm), a
do-it-yourself software application for creating a store that can
be posted on Open Market's own servers, or any other World
Wide Web (WWW) site on the Internet. StoreBuilder, initially
available in an on-line version, has an easy-to-use interface
that guides a prospective merchant through the creation of a
complete on-line store, including product and service
descriptions, pricing and store policy information, plus a secure
payment system. 

"The rules for success in the electronic marketplace are very
different than in traditional commerce," said Shikhar Ghosh,
co-founder and chief executive officer of the company. "The
process of creating products, attracting customers, accepting
payment and providing ongoing services requires a completely
new approach. Open Market is the first to provide a total
infrastructure to create and support this new on-line frontier. 

"By automating the process of putting a business on-line, we will
enable thousands of merchants to get onto the Internet. And by
providing the tools for creating new types of products, we enable
them to bring a higher level of value to their customers," he said. 

David Gifford, co-founder and chairman of Open Market, and a
pioneer in the creation of security and information management
systems in open networks added, "We are committed to making it
as easy as possible for companies and individuals to participate
in the electronic marketplace. 

"Open Market's expertise in integrating advanced technologies
such as agent-based searching, smart card-based
authentication and various Web technologies is creating an
infrastructure that will empower businesses to capitalize on the
opportunities in electronic commerce," he said. 

Open Market also announced today that it had established a
number of strategic relationships with companies that are
utilizing the company's system to sell goods and services over
the Internet. They include: Digital Equipment Corporation, a
leader in using the Internet for electronic commerce, which today
announced its intention to sell the OMI solution to financial
institutions and other businesses; Ipswitch, Inc., a TCP/IP
software solutions provider, which is using StoreBuilder to
distribute software, product upgrades and enhancements to its
customers on-line, and Mead Data Central, which is using the
system to offer new LEXIS(R)/NEXIS(R) services to customers over
the Internet. 

Over the next three months, a number of other leading providers
of information products, information services and hard goods
will announce plans to deploy the Open Market system. 

"What these projects have in common," said Ghosh, "is that they
take advantage of the unique ability of Open Market's system to
change the way services are sold and presented to customers. For
example, Open Market's technology makes it practical and
cost-effective for an information services provider to offer a
research report by the page, by the chapter, or by the sentence. It
enables textbook publishers to create and merchandise a
dynamic textbook which can be updated continuously to reflect
the most current advances in the field. And it makes it
economical for software companies to distribute software on-line
and provide customers with upgrades on a continuous basis," he
said. 

The Merchant Server
===================

Open Market's system consists of two major interconnected
components: a Merchant Server and a Payment Server. 

The Merchant Server provides an infrastructure of tools and
services required to create and manage an on-line business, plus
a sophisticated interface which enables buyers to browse, select,
and pay for goods and services. It also includes the StoreBuilder
kit, which gives merchants a simple process to create an Internet
storefront that can be placed on Open Market's electronic
marketplace, or any other site on the World Wide Web. The kit
offers templates that the merchant uses to describe the business;
list the products or services offered; provide information on price
and availability; outline service and support policies, and present
other information. StoreBuilder also enables a merchant to
manage inventory by simply adding or deleting products, and to
catalog and index the offerings so that they are available to any
Internet shopper. The new store automatically connects to Open
Market's secure payment system. 

Using StoreBuilder, a merchant can create an on-line store for an
introductory price of less than $1,500, plus an ongoing monthly
fee based on size of store and number of transactions. 

Shrink-wrapped personal computer versions of the software will
be available early next year for merchants without access to an
Internet connection. Open Market is also establishing a network
of authorized agents to help merchants establish an on-line
store. 

The Payment Server
==================

The Payment Server supports customer service and account
management capabilities, payment and authentication, plus a
number of unique security features. Open Market's payment
system accepts credit cards, and later will be extended to support
other standard payment methods such as debit cards, ACH
transactions, corporate accounts and digital cash. The system
uses multiple security mechanisms, including encryption,
passwords, challenge-based PINs and smart cards to create
secure transactions. The security mechanism is based on the risk
of an individual transaction, thus a $1 transaction goes through
a lower level of security than a $2,000 wire transfer. 

The payment system is fully integrated with accounting,
customer service and dispute resolution mechanisms. Customers
can set their own transaction limits, view their statements
on-line, review items they purchased, and query the merchant
through the system. Unlike other encryption-based schemes,
Open Market's solution supports any standard through
forms-based browser such as Mosaic. 

Open Market's system also incorporates a number of other
features for buyers and merchants, including: 

 o Pay-per-page - the ability to cost-effectively sell and bill
   customers for information in any desired increment, i.e., by
   the paragraph, chapter, or book; 

 o Pay-per-time - the ability to sell subscriptions to
   information or services for varying lengths of time. For
   example, an on-line training company can offer courses in
   packages ranging from one hour to one year, depending on
   the complexity of the subject matter; 

 o Document fingerprinting - the capability to uniquely
   stamp and number information to discourage
   unauthorized copying and reselling; 

 o Smart reports(tm) - reports that provide merchants with
   detail on store traffic and sales volume, average time spent
   browsing, and other information about store operations; 

 o Customer feedback mechanism - a way to easily and
   instantaneously communicate between merchant and
   buyers; 

 o Smart statements(tm) - detailed interactive reports for
   buyers containing a complete and permanent history of
   when and how purchases were made. 

 o Personalized home page - a feature which enables buyers
   to execute complex searches or receive digests of news.
   Sophisticated agent-based technology and searching
   capability will be added over time to enhance this feature; 

 o Shopping cart - a feature which enables a buyer to collect
   information from multiple Open Market merchants, and
   post it one place in order to compare features or price
   before making a final purchase decision; 

 o Multi-vendor theme store - a feature, available in a
   subsequent release, which enables the creation of a
   one-time Internet store selected from goods and services
   provided by other Open Market merchants. For example, a
   buyer planning a wedding could configure a store
   containing caterers, photographers, flower vendors,
   invitation printers, and other services required, select the
   suppliers, and make the purchases. 

About Open Market, Inc.
=======================

Open Market was founded by David Gifford and Shikhar Ghosh
in 1994 to develop and market systems and tools to facilitate
electronic commerce. Professor Gifford heads the Programming
Systems Research Group at the Massachusetts Institute of
Technology. 

Co-founder Ghosh previously was chief executive officer of
Appex Corporation, a company that established a payment
system, clearing house and call validation infrastructure for the
cellular telephone industry. 

Open Market has exclusive rights to certain security, wide-area
search and agent-based information retrieval technology
developed at MIT. This technology creates the foundation for the
next generation of electronic commerce on other media, such as
cable TV, by making it easy for consumers to find the products
they need and providing the ability to get searches done for them
from their computers. 

Information on Open Market and its products and services can be
obtained by sending electronic mail to pressinfo@openmarket.com. The
company is located at 215 First St., Cambridge, Mass., 02142. The
telephone number is (617) 621-9500, and the fax number is (617)
621-1703. Open Market's World Wide Web address (URL) is
http://www.openmarket.com.

